<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-similar-to-diagonalizable"><p>
	Suppose that <m> A </m> and <m> B </m> are <term><xref ref="definition-diagonalization-similar" text="title" /></term>.
	Prove that if <m> B </m> is diagonalizable,
	then <m> A </m> must be as well.
</p></exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-reordering-eigenvalues">
	<title>Reordering eigenvalues</title>

	<statement>

		<p>
		A <m> 4 \times 4 </m> matrix <m> A </m> with characteristic polynomial <md> c_A(\lambda) = (\lambda - 1) (\lambda - 2) (\lambda - 3) (\lambda - 4) </md> must be diagonalizable,
		by <xref ref="corollary-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity-diag-n-distinct-evalues">Statement</xref>
		of <xref ref="corollary-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity-diag" />.
		In particular,
		if <m> P </m> is a <m> 4 \times 4 </m> matrix constructed by an eigenvector for each of the eigenvalues <m> \lambda = 1, 2, 3, 4 </m>,
		in order,
		for its columns,
		then it will be that <md> \inv{P} A P = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ \amp 2 \\ \amp \amp 3 \\ \amp \amp \amp 4 \end{bmatrix} </md>.
		</p>

		<p>
		Determine an invertible matrix <m> E </m> so that,
		for <m> Q = P E </m>,
		it will be that
		<md> \inv{Q} A Q = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ \amp 2 \\ \amp \amp 1 \\ \amp \amp \amp 4 \end{bmatrix} </md>.
		</p>

	</statement>

	<hint><p>
		The letter <m> E </m> has been chosen deliberately to represent the matrix to be used to <q>transform</q> the initial transition matrix <m> P </m> into a new transition matrix <m> Q </m>.
		Recall that the result of a matrix product <m> E P </m>,
		with an <term><xref ref="definition-elem-matrices" text="title" /></term> <m> E </m> on the <em> left </em>,
		is the same as if an elementary row operation had been performed on <m> P </m>.
		It turns out that the result of a matrix product <m> P E </m> with elementary <m> E </m> on the <em> right </em> is the same as if an <q>elementary <em>column</em> operation</q> had been performed on <m> P </m>.
		Apply this new pattern for elementary matrices,
		combined with the summary information in the concluding sentences of <xref ref="procedure-diagonalization-concepts-diag" />
	</p></hint>

	<answer><p><m>\displaystyle
		E =
		\begin{bmatrix}
			0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
			1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
		\end{bmatrix}
	</m></p></answer>

</exercise>

<!-- Exercise group -->
<xi:include href="./exercises.d/similar-diag.ptx" />

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-pattern-similar-diag">
	<title>Pattern of similarity for diagonal matrices</title>

	<task xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-pattern-similar-diag-initial"><p>
		Based on the exercises under the heading <xref ref="exercisegroup-diagonalization-similar-diag" text="title" /> above,
		make a conjecture about the precise condition(s) under which two diagonal matrices of the same size are similar.
	</p></task>

	<task xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-pattern-similar-diag-reword"><p>
		In light of <xref ref="proposition-eigen-values-vectors-special-forms" />,
		reword your answer to <xref ref="exercise-diagonalization-pattern-similar-diag-initial" text="type-local" />
		to <em>no longer</em> use the term <term>diagonal entries</term>,
		but using the terms
		<term>eigenvalues</term> and <term><xref ref="definition-diagonalization-alg-mult" text="title" /></term> instead.
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		Does the pattern of similarity between diagonal matrices,
		expressed using your reworded formulation
		from <xref ref="exercise-diagonalization-pattern-similar-diag-reword" text="type-local" />,
		also accurately characterize when two <em>diagonalizable</em> (but not necessarily diagonal) matrices are similar?
	</p></task>

	<task>

		<statement><p>
			More generally,
			does the pattern of similarity from <xref ref="exercise-diagonalization-pattern-similar-diag-reword" text="type-local" />
			also accurately characterize when <em> any </em> two (not necessarily diagonal nor diagonalizable) matrices are similar?
		</p></statement>

		<hint><p>
			Review the following:
			<ul>

				<li>
					The comparison of the results of <xref ref="activity-diagonalization-diag-proc" /> and <xref ref="activity-diagonalization-non-diag-ex" />
					that you filled out in <xref ref="activity-diagonalization-compare-diag-and-non-diag" />.
				</li>

				<li> <xref ref="exercise-diagonalization-similar-to-diagonalizable" text="type-local" />. </li>

			</ul>

		</p></hint>

	</task>

</exercise>

<!-- Exercise group -->
<xi:include href="./exercises.d/determine-similar.ptx" />

<exercise>
	<title>Similarity of upper/lower triangular</title>

	<statement><p>
		Verify that for every upper triangular matrix there exists a lower triangular matrix to which it is similar.
	</p></statement>

	<hint><p>
		Reconsider the information in the hint to <xref ref="exercise-diagonalization-reordering-eigenvalues" text="type-local" /> in the following way:
		if <m> E </m> is an elementary matrix,
		then the result of <m> \inv{E} A E </m> can be re-interpreted as applying both an elementary <em> column </em> operation and the corresponding <em> reverse </em> elementary <em> row </em> operation.
	</p></hint>

	<solution><p>
		Use the <q>anti-diagonal</q> transition matrix
		<m>
			P =
			\left[\begin{smallmatrix}
				  \amp   \amp         \amp 1 \\
				  \amp   \amp \iddots        \\
				  \amp 1                     \\
				1
			\end{smallmatrix}\right]
		</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercisegroup>
	<title>Similarity of scalar matrices</title>

	<introduction><p>
		In each case,
		verify that the matrix is similar to <em> no </em> other matrix besides itself.
	</p></introduction>

	<exercise><p> The <m> n \times n </m> identity matrix. </p></exercise>

	<exercise><p> The <m> n \times n </m> zero matrix. </p></exercise>

	<exercise><p> An  <m> n \times n </m> <xref ref="definition-special-forms-scalar-matrix" text="title" />. </p></exercise>

</exercisegroup>

